# Paths the harness owns inside this project root. Anything not listed is the product.
#
# Two classes, and the rule they exist for:
#   [mechanism] = committed with the project and shipped. The harness code, its boot files, its
#                 contracts, its self-tests (under alpaca/, at alpaca/tests/), pytest.ini and project.yaml.
#                 A copy or an upgrade refreshes these; they are identical in every copied project.
#                 setup/ship.py stages exactly this list, so it is also the shipment allowlist.
#   [memory]    = runtime state, gitignored. The record (.alpaca/), the rendered RESUME.md, the
#                 analytics/ projection and the local environment. This is one project's own state.
#
# The contamination rule: the memory class must never travel to another project. It is this
# project's own runtime state, which is why a used copy is never the copy source. Copying the
# harness into an existing repo surfaces every colliding mechanism path to the owner instead of
# writing over it (see alpaca/manifest.py collisions); the memory class is never a collision and
# never a copy source.
#
# P-001 keeps project.yaml in [mechanism] (human-owned, carries the project id analytics keys on).
# P-002 moves the self-tests under alpaca/tests/ (so they cannot collide with a product's own tests/)
# and takes design/ and docs/ out of the mechanism class as whole directories; the operating
# guides under docs/ are listed one by one below, and design/ stays in the repo as prose only.
